    "A": "Explore representing obstacles sparsely by row without sorting: preserve input coordinates in per-row lists and test membership efficiently or use a compact mark structure. Compare against the measured dense contiguous byte grid and avoid added preprocessing that outweighs savings. Keep the one-row recurrence, exact modulo behavior, and correctness for duplicate/arbitrary blocked coordinates; benchmark the complete executable.",
    "B": "Investigate a sparse-obstacle algorithm that skips long unblocked spans using binomial coefficients modulo 1,000,000,007, e.g. obstacle-point DP with inclusion-exclusion over sorted coordinates. The input guarantees few obstacles for large N, while dense O(N²) scanning dominates there. Handle duplicates and blocked endpoints correctly, and retain a general fallback if advantageous. Validate on all inputs and benchmark end-to-end, including preprocessing.",
    "C": "Independently explore optimization of the dense-grid path: compare byte/bit obstacle representations and row-wise traversal, and assess whether blocked-cell checks or modulo additions dominate. The one-dimensional DP already removes row copies and measured near 0.049 s, so seek a modest, evidence-based gain without introducing sorting overhead or compromising arbitrary valid inputs. Benchmark full runs and validate correctness.",
    "G": "Integrate only correctness-validated candidate programs from A/B/C. The best measured input is role B at 0.048936 s, narrowly ahead of C (0.049254 s) and A's contiguous-byte version (0.049790 s); all improve the 0.081191 s baseline. Treat this as a starting point, not proof that changes compose: benchmark integrated combinations on the official harness, preserve correctness for every valid input (including duplicate obstacles and blocked endpoints), and retain the best measured valid version. Do not assume additive speedups.",
    "analysis": "Problem D's baseline allocates and scans an N×N blocked grid and uses two DP rows plus a row-copy pass. Measurements show the shared in-place one-row DP and fast streams reduce mean time substantially, with B fastest among admissible variants. Dense work remains O(N²), whereas the statement makes obstacle counts sparse for large N, motivating an independent sparse-coordinate approach. Sorting-per-row had a worse measured time (0.0551 s), cautioning against preprocessing overhead; contiguous byte storage alone also did not beat B. Research should benchmark whole-program performance and preserve modulo path counts for all valid grids."
